WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.160
OK, and so getting to
this week's end today's

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.060
lecture, so from a user roadmap,
over the last few weeks,

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.920
we started looking
at modern ways

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.680
to approach
learning-based control.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.360
We started from imitation
learning where the idea there

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.000
was to learn to mimic the
behavior of an expert.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.990
And then last week we
started to introduce

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.240
reinforcement learning, which
is this idea of learning

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.560
through trial and error.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.400
And specifically, we
introduce some foundational

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.920
learning mechanisms to do so.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.520
And starting today and for the
next lectures, for the remaining

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.320
lectures of this
class, we'll be looking

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.560
at specific categories
of algorithms,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.640
today starting from
value based methods

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.640
that belong to the category
of model-free reinforcement

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.640
learning methods.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.580
So briefly mention a couple
of amazing references.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.880
Of course, you all know probably
Sutton and Barto's textbooks

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.420
on reinforcement learning
but also discourse

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.440
by David Silver, which is great.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.060
And also some of
the slides today

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.380
are indeed inspired
by that course.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:14.500
So to briefly pick up what
we were saying last week,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.600
so remember that in
previous lectures,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.540
we basically started to make
the distinction between what

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.740
we refer to as prediction,
whereby given a policy that

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.220
was given to us we
wanted to estimate

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.220
the value for that policy and
control, whereby the goal which

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.120
is actually what we
deeply care about,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.220
is to learn the
policy that optimizes

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.780
some measure of performance.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.580
And specifically, we started
looking at algorithms motivated

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.460
by dynamic programming,
which we refer

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.540
to as exact methods,
specifically policy iteration

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.020
and value iterations as ways
to solve Markov decision

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.100
processes, which is indeed
the mathematical formalism we

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.680
are using also for
reinforcement training.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.020
And we also highlighted
how these models,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.340
despite being great at solving
Markov decision processes,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.120
had one main limitation,
which is the one that in order

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.160
to use them, we assumed we
have a perfect knowledge

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.720
of the system dynamics.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.520
Specifically, we have knowledge
of the transition function,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.720
t, which is the probability
of going in state xt plus 1

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:24.120
given in state xt
given action, ut.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.500
And so, basically,
last time last week

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.160
we started to introduce two
really fundamental learning

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.542
paradigms to which
really encompass

00:02:33.542 --> 00:02:35.000
this idea of
reinforcement learning

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.820
which is learning
from interaction.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.540
So we don't have access
to a dynamical model.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.200
We want to learn without
it through interactions

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.360
from the environment.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.440
And we saw specifically
Monte Carlo learning

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.120
and temporal difference learning
as two key instantiations

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.380
of these learning paradigms.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.120
We started building
out, even visually,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.660
a mental map of
different approaches

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.820
to learning from
interaction, whereby

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.460
we were looking at these
diagrams which essentially

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.940
represent rollouts
in our environments.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.920
So if you recall,
in these diagrams,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.100
white nodes represent
states and black nodes

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:11.940
represent possible
actions that we could

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.860
take in that specific state.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.380
And so these basically
visualize this idea

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.380
of the possible futures that we
could have in our environment.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.900
And ideally, when we
look at estimating

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.880
the value of a state
given a policy,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.860
we are potentially
ultimately interested

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.060
in estimating the expected
value of all the leaves

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.560
in our possible rollouts.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.300
So this is the actual
quantity that we're

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.700
interested in estimating.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.860
Of course, one way assuming
we have infinite compute

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.660
would be to just simulate
our environment infinitely

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.820
many times forward, collect
the actual expectations of all

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.760
of our leaf nodes, and
estimate the values like this.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.080
But then we saw how
dynamic programming gave us

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.780
through the principle
of optimality

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.720
gave us a practical way of
approximating and calculating,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:08.320
actually, these values through
basically a one step calculation

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.440
whereby the value
of a given state

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.200
was essentially defined as
the expectation of the reward

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.279
that we observe in the
one step transition

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:21.480
plus the discounted future
value at our next state.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.080
The catch here is that to
compute those expectations

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.800
exactly, we needed the knowledge
of the transition function

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.140
or our dynamics.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.920
And so this is where Monte Carlo
and temporal difference learning

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.380
came in place, whereby we
used sampling, that is,

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.720
we simulated some rollouts
in our environment.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.200
And in Monte Carlo, we update
our guess of our value function

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.840
by taking a step in the
direction of the return, gt,

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.720
where we calculate
this gt simply

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.620
by having a rollout
in our environment

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.060
until we reach a terminal
state and summing up

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.740
all of the rewards that we
observed in this rollout.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:06.260
In td, on the other hand,
we define the target

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:10.320
by basically getting leveraging
ideas from both Monte Carlo,

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.800
that is, the sampling
and dynamic programming,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.820
that is the
bootstrapping, by defining

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:21.460
the target as this one step
estimate of the final return.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.420
And so we saw how compared
to dynamic programming, which

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:28.180
has no need to sample because
we have the underlying dynamics,

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:31.100
Monte Carlo and temporal
difference learning both sample

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.940
because we do have interactions
in our environment.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:39.180
And we saw how TD and
dynamic programming

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.900
bootstrap because they guess
towards another guess, which

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.020
is our estimate at the
next time step, t plus 1.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.620
Well, Monte Carlo does not
bootstrap because it actually

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.280
rolls out until the
end of the episode.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.040
Of course, we
introduced everything

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.920
in this slide for the purpose
of estimating value functions

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.720
or state value functions, v.
But everything that we just said

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:07.600
holds equally for state action
value functions or Q functions.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.095
For control-- yes.

00:06:10.095 --> 00:06:13.080
In Monte Carlo you always
have to go to a terminal state

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.360
or is there any where you
go and you take five steps

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.560
and then reevaluate?

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.600
For Monte Carlo, you always need
to reach a terminal state, which

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.720
is indeed, if you wish, one of
the limitations of Monte Carlo.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.440
But we discussed trade offs
between bias and variance

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:27.600
of these estimators.

00:06:31.895 --> 00:06:34.020
But so this is great for
the purpose of prediction.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.500
That is estimating
values given a policy.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.720
But what we actually
care about is

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.980
finding those optimal
policies themselves.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.480
So we care about control.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.240
And so we discussed
a general paradigm

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.420
for control, which is
one of generalized policy

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:53.420
iteration, whereby
the main idea here

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:56.600
is that we are alternating
between two different processes.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.460
So we're given a arbitrarily
initialized value

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.060
function and a policy.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.680
And we alternate between
policy evaluation step.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.100
That is, given the policy, we
estimate the value function

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:10.440
of that policy and a
policy improvement step,

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.060
which, given the
updated value function,

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:17.360
kind of uses it to carve out
a better policy out of it.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.600
And by iterating through
these two processes,

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.460
we hopefully converge
to the optimal value

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.980
function and associated policy.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:29.500
Specifically, we saw kind of a
v0 version of a working control

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.020
algorithm, which we refer to
as Monte Carlo control, which

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:36.580
is basically alternating between
two specific instantiations

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:40.540
of the policy evaluation process
and the policy improvement

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.900
process.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.020
For policy evaluation, we are
essentially using a Monte Carlo

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:48.120
estimate of a Q function.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.160
Well, for policy
improvement, we assume

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.760
to do an epsilon greedy
policy, if you recall.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.580
So again, we saw this
general formalism.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.560
And then we had
a v0 RL algorithm

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.880
which we denoted as
Monte Carlo control where

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.720
we had two specific
instantiations of policy

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.260
evaluation and
policy improvement.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:14.080
So today what we will
essentially be doing

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.480
is we'll be building on
top of these concepts

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:23.178
by, first of all, introducing
two very popular algorithms

00:08:23.178 --> 00:08:25.720
like falling between the family
of value-based methods, which

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.040
is Sarsa and Q-learning.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.480
We'll use the discussion
on these two algorithms

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.000
to deep dive and
explore it a bit more

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.919
in depth the difference between
on-policy and off-policy

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:36.780
algorithms.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.120
We'll see more shortly.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.320
And then we'll introduce value
function approximation as a way

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.780
to scale these algorithms
to higher dimensional state

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.980
and action spaces.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.540
And we'll conclude
with a brief mentions

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.340
to some deep
reinforcement learning

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:53.960
methods and applications.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.340
All good with the plan?

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.900
Nice.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:09.120
OK, so we basically discussed
Monte Carlo and TD learning.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.260
And last week we mentioned how
temporal difference learning

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.740
essentially has, if you wish,
several advantages over Monte

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.100
Carlo learning to some extent.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:23.060
The first one is that we saw how
TD learning is a lower variance

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:26.260
estimate of the
ground truth return.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.820
So remember that we were making
this even visual distinction

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.140
whereby assuming we have a
rollout in our environment

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.640
until we reach some
terminal state at time t.

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:47.830
In Monte Carlo, to estimate the
expected return for our policy,

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:52.090
we would essentially be
rolling out the entire episode

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:56.890
until we are able to compute
our target return, gt,

00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:01.850
which is simply the discounted
sum of all the rewards

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.350
that we observed over time.

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:09.170
And we saw how this target,
which in Monte Carlo

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:10.570
is simply the return.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:16.690
For TD, we basically used the
kind of a dynamic programming

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:20.730
estimate whereby
our target becomes

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:26.130
the instantaneous reward plus
the discounted future value.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:28.930
And we last time we basically
highlighted the fact

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:33.130
that by looking at the amount of
stochasticity that is involved,

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:36.390
TD learning is a lower variance
estimator because it simply put,

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:39.370
we're sampling only once
while in Monte Carlo in order

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.530
to estimate all the way
down here in our episode

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.190
we would have to account for
the added stochasticity that

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:49.390
comes by sampling
in our environment

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:53.110
further along the
way, essentially.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:56.350
Not only that, we also discussed
that since in TD learning we

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:59.750
are working with
one step updates,

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.210
we can use this update online.

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:05.090
That is, as we progress
in our rollout,

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:08.710
we could keep updating our
estimates of the value function.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:13.150
And because we don't
need a terminal episode,

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:17.190
we can work with incomplete
sequences, essentially.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:22.150
So long story short, we
have a number of advantages

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:23.370
from TD learning.

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:27.370
So kind of a natural
idea would be,

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:32.130
why don't we just replace in our
Monte Carlo control algorithm?

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:37.630
That is our v0 RL algorithm TD
learning where we previously

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.910
had Monte Carlo learning
of our Q function.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:48.050
And basically,
this is essentially

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:51.450
the step that leads us from our
Monte Carlo control algorithm

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:54.610
to what is known as Sarsa.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:57.210
So let us look a
little bit more closely

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:00.250
at what it means to update
action value functions or Q

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:04.370
functions within this
algorithm known as Sarsa.

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:06.850
So specifically,
we referred before

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.890
to the temporal
difference backup

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:11.770
as updating our guess
of our value function

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:16.330
by taking a step
towards the TD target.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:21.850
Of course, this holds for value
functions, v, but equally for Q

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:24.330
functions in the same way.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:28.610
Of course, the moment we want
to define this TD backup for Q

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:32.730
functions, we need a
few specific elements,

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:37.010
which are essentially we need
the current state and control,

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:39.070
xt and ut.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:42.630
We need the reward that are
obtained from those by taking

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.350
that control on that state.

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.830
And then we need the
future state, xt,

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:54.670
and the future control
that we take at t plus 1.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:56.630
But once we have this,
this is essentially

00:12:56.630 --> 00:13:04.550
what defines the Q function
update in this algorithm.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:07.910
So specifically, now
just a note on notation,

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:10.350
in reinforcement
learning literature,

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:13.110
this quintuple of
events is usually

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:16.350
expressed with states
represented as S

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.870
and actions represented
as A. And so

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:20.530
if you take this
quintuple of events,

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.250
which are the ones that we need
in order to define this update,

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:24.865
this literally spells out Sarsa.

00:13:24.865 --> 00:13:26.990
And so this is also where
the name of the algorithm

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:28.710
comes from.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.830
But again, kind of simply
put, at a high level, what

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:35.430
we're doing is that now we
took our Monte Carlo control

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:36.410
algorithm.

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:40.850
We replaced Monte Carlo
evaluation of the Q function

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:45.210
with TD updates
of the Q function.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.850
And we're doing this
at every time step

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.190
because this is something
that TD allows us to do.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:58.890
And so indeed, you see that
the step from what we're

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:02.170
doing to arguably one of
the most famous algorithms

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.770
out there, Sarsa, is
indeed pretty small.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:10.050
And so I think it's useful to
go through pseudocode to really

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.650
ground our intuition.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:17.150
And so this is, if you wish,
how Sarsa is loosely defined.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.850
So we initialize a Q function.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:24.530
Again, this could be all
zeros at the beginning.

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:27.490
And we start our learning loop.

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:30.250
So we are initially
initialized in our environment

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:32.570
at some state, xt.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.150
At that state xt,
we choose an action

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:39.350
according to our greedy policy
or epsilon greedy policy.

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:45.150
So remember that we
discussed our policy, pi.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:47.270
If we choose an
epsilon greedy policy,

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:50.990
is something that for
with probability epsilon,

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:54.270
we'll choose a random action.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.510
And with probability
1 minus epsilon,

00:14:56.510 --> 00:15:03.130
we'll instead choose the argmax
over u of our Q function.

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:09.970
So we are in a given state.

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.030
We act according to our
epsilon greedy policy

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:14.910
and pick an action, u.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:17.670
This will lead us into--
will basically give us

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.390
a reward for that
action and next state

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:25.470
from which we repeat the
selection of our control, ut

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:27.910
plus 1 according to our
epsilon greedy policy.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:31.670
And now we basically collected
all of our five elements

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.090
that we need for our update.

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:38.490
So we have the initial
state and control, xt, ut,

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:41.250
we have the reward
associated with this control,

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:45.090
and the following xt
plus 1, ut plus 1.

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:48.650
And so here we just simply
compute this update rule

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:51.930
that we defined
previously, which

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:56.870
is nothing else than the
TD backup for Q functions.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:01.770
Of course, here the
clock ticks and we

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.630
can repeat until we converge.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:12.750
Any questions here?

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:13.670
Does it make sense?

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:24.530
Yes.

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:25.370
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:28.910
Yes, TD.

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:33.030
So here we're using TD
version of our update

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:36.790
for the Q functions, where
we are updating our value

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:40.587
functions according to
basically one step look ahead,

00:16:40.587 --> 00:16:41.170
you might say.

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:43.150
So we're taking one
step in our environment.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:46.970
And as we were showing here
in our pictorial diagrams,

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:49.550
we're essentially using
only this one step update

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.370
to update our values.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:01.810
So let's dig a
little bit deeper.

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:04.030
And let's build some
intuition into what

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:05.450
we're actually doing here.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:08.470
And what are the results from
the perspective of the policy

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.310
that we actually learned?

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:12.470
And so let's go through this.

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:15.630
What I'm referring here as
a windy gridworld example.

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.510
So here we are in
a gridworld where

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:20.150
the goal is, as usual, to
go from a starting cell

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:23.190
to a goal cell.

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:27.190
The policy or the action
space is basically

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:30.250
the four cardinal directions.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:32.650
And in this grid
world, the catch

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:35.690
is that we assume we have
some kind of fictitious wind

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:43.690
that is coming from below, which
basically pushes the agent, x

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:47.770
cells up depending where x
is the value encoded here

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:49.650
below each of these columns.

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:51.890
So, for example, if
we were in this column

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.915
here in the third one,
we would have no wind.

00:17:54.915 --> 00:17:56.290
If we were in the
fourth, we will

00:17:56.290 --> 00:18:00.210
be pushed one up, and so forth.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:01.930
As usual, the goal
that we want to solve

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:04.810
is to basically come
up with the policy that

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:07.070
reaches the goal in the
minimum time possible,

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:11.330
and we encode a minimum time
through a stage wise reward that

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:17.370
is negative 1 for every time
our clock ticks, essentially,

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:20.490
and we have some
specific hyperparameters.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.190
Epsilon refers to our
epsilon greedy policy.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.290
Alpha is a step
size of our update,

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:28.690
and we pick a gamma of 1.

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:34.510
So now even just by
eyeballing this example,

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.010
how would you describe
the optimal policy here?

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:43.510
Yeah.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.710
The bottom part of
the thing before you

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:49.990
move to the right [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:51.130
This is a good intuition.

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:54.710
It's actually not
correct in this case.

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:57.870
Can anyone spot why?

00:18:57.870 --> 00:18:58.510
Yeah.

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.010
You can't move over and
up in the same grid.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:02.570
So you can go all
the way to bottom,

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:06.090
but to push you over g by
the time you get there.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:06.930
Yeah, exactly.

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:10.230
Because if you look closely
at the intensity of the window

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:12.890
here, if you're way all
the way to the bottom here,

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:14.650
you'll basically go up here.

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:16.910
Then you take one step
and you go up again.

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:18.730
You take one step,
and you go up again.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:20.750
And then here we're
taking two steps.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:22.380
So you won't be able to reach.

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.630
So actually, it turns out
that the optimal thing to do

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:29.410
would be to just push yourself
to the ceiling and then go back.

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:33.150
So here go back here and then
ultimately go to your goal.

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:40.473
So here's where I ask you
to do a leap of faith.

00:19:40.473 --> 00:19:41.890
But if you actually
implement this

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:43.850
in practice, what
you will see is

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:46.090
something that looks very
similar to what is here

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:50.250
on the slide, whereby
the first of all, Sarsa

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:53.450
indeed does converge to the
optimal policy, which is

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:57.170
similar to how we described it.

00:19:57.170 --> 00:20:00.070
And by looking at the
training dynamics,

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:03.450
we will see something similar
to this plot on the slide.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.850
Specifically, this plot is
showing on the x-axis the time

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:09.110
steps of interactions
with the environment,

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:10.810
so literally how many
time steps we are

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:12.890
observing in our environment.

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:18.270
And on the y-axis, we have the
number of successful episodes,

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.050
meaning how many times do
we actually reach the goal?

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:24.070
So you will see that
initially our Q function

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:27.390
is, and therefore our policy,
is randomly initialized.

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:30.850
So we'll basically be
succeeding 0 times.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:34.170
So we never reach the
goal until at some point,

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:38.790
even just by random exploration,
we will reach the goal.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:43.310
And here's where the
magic of the TD backup

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:45.690
start to happen because
through those backups,

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:51.030
we're basically starting to back
propagate basically positivity

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:53.670
and the goodness of our
decisions all the way

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:55.550
back to the starting point.

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:58.110
And so you will see that
over time the performance

00:20:58.110 --> 00:20:59.410
of the policy improves.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.050
Does the graphs here make sense?

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.910
Now, would you say that--

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:15.150
within this
environment, would you

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:18.090
say that Monte Carlo methods
would also easily apply?

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:26.130
And in either case,
why do you think so?

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.410
This is a more subtle
question, but I think

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:31.070
it's a good one to think about.

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:32.570
Yes.

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:34.612
It wouldn't be the case
because you would try out

00:21:34.612 --> 00:21:35.987
a bunch of different
combinations

00:21:35.987 --> 00:21:38.230
and you only terminate if
once you reach your goal.

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.870
So the goal is that you
spend a lot of downtime,

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:44.250
just kind of wander around until
eventually it lands on the goal?

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:45.250
Yeah, yeah.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:48.370
I would say this is
definitely the right intuition

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:51.010
because in Monte Carlo
methods, remember we

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:54.050
require terminating episodes.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:55.930
Well, in this case,
terminating episodes

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:57.970
is only when we reach the goal.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:00.530
So we might be
lucky in the sense

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:05.250
that we end up into some
random exploration that

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:06.350
does reach the goal.

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:07.810
But in the general
case, it is not

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.350
guaranteed that we'll
be able to do so.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:15.970
So I think a nice
counterexample to think about

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:23.130
is say that in our
grid world example,

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:29.830
we have a situation whereby for
some behavior of our learning

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:33.230
dynamics, we converge to
a temporary policy that

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:40.150
is something like this,
something where we have a circle

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:42.590
loop in our policy.

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:46.070
Then in Monte Carlo,
this will essentially

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:47.650
never update the Q function.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:49.950
And it will be
stuck in this loop.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:53.230
On the other hand, if we
are in the same situation

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:58.310
with TD learning, TD learning
allows us to update online.

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:01.350
So at each step, we are actually
updating the values over

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:03.230
in our Q function.

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:04.870
And so this means
that if we were

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:07.650
to be stuck in this circle
loops, at some point,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:12.190
we are basically accruing
negative rewards over time.

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:15.510
And so this would mean that
these specific behaviors

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.790
at some point will be actually
worse than doing something else.

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:34.010
Indeed, and so it's exactly
as we said in the sense

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:36.490
that termination is not
guaranteed for all policies

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:39.630
here, and therefore
kind of success of MC,

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:41.630
Monte Carlo learning, is
not guaranteed as well.

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:49.310
So we now introduce,
if you wish,

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:52.890
one of the first
complete reinforcement

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:54.930
learning algorithms,
namely Sarsa.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:59.130
Let's use Sarsa as a kind
of grounding algorithm

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.330
to start discussing
the differences

00:24:01.330 --> 00:24:04.050
between on-policy and
off-policy learning.

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:06.310
So we mentioned
it briefly before,

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:09.170
but let us briefly revisit
also the definitions of what

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:12.410
we mean by on-policy
and off-policy learning.

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:14.510
We'll dig more later on this.

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:16.710
But this is at a
high level how I

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:19.550
would describe on-policy
and off-policy learning.

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:22.110
So for on-policy,
we mean that we

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:25.590
are interested in
evaluating or improving

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:28.230
the policy that is currently
used to make decisions

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:30.510
in the environment.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:32.890
On the other hand,
for off-policy,

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:35.830
we are interested in
evaluating or improving

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:38.190
a policy that might be
different from the one that

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.150
is used to generate data, that
is the one that is actively

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:44.008
interacting with
the environment.

00:24:44.008 --> 00:24:46.050
Again, we'll formalize
this a little bit shortly.

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:51.230
But I think this is intuitively
the main difference.

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:56.510
Now, with these two kind
of loose definitions,

00:24:56.510 --> 00:25:00.230
in which of these two buckets
would you place Sarsa?

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:03.450
And again, here, why
would you do that?

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:16.140
It's also your policy
because you're [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:23.450
So we are modifying it.

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:26.370
But I would argue
that we are modifying

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:30.350
the policy that is actively
being used in the environment.

00:25:30.350 --> 00:25:33.490
So I'm giving away the
answer a little bit.

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:36.530
But in this case and
in general, Sarsa

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:39.170
is to be considered
an on policy algorithm

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:42.410
because if you look at the
pseudocode a little bit more

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.330
closely, we are
defining our policy

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:46.930
as the epsilon greedy policy.

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:50.610
And we are indeed improving
this same epsilon greedy policy.

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:54.810
So the policy will change
over time, and you're correct.

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:57.970
But we are indeed
going to be improving

00:25:57.970 --> 00:26:00.370
the same policy that is
being used to interact

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:01.430
with the environment.

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:11.650
So yeah, Sarsa is indeed
a non-policy algorithm.

00:26:11.650 --> 00:26:14.270
But so let us now dig
a little bit deeper

00:26:14.270 --> 00:26:17.590
into off-policy learning.

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:20.330
How do we define it
slightly more formally?

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:23.870
And also why do we ultimately
care about off-policy learning?

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:27.590
So, in general,
off-policy learning

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:31.110
can be seen as the mechanism
through which we want

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:35.990
to evaluate a target policy pi.

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:40.110
That is, we want to compute
the associated value functions

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:45.150
for this target policy pi while
following a behavior policy, mu,

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:46.950
that is a different policy.

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:52.490
Specifically, the data we'll use
to define our update equations

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:55.390
will basically be
sampled by interactions

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:58.710
in the environment that
are collected by following

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.430
the behavior policy, mu.

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:05.250
So the data here is obtained by
observing a different policy.

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:14.330
So why would being able to do
something like this be useful?

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:23.730
So, first of all, in some cases,
for example, robotics to be one,

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.930
we might not have the
luxury of actually

00:27:26.930 --> 00:27:30.690
interact with the real world or
with the environment infinitely

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:31.470
many times.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.890
Maybe in some cases,
we can only learn

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:37.650
through either observations
of humans or other agents

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:38.770
doing things.

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:42.330
And therefore having
this framework whereby

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:47.690
we are able to evaluate and
learn about a different policy

00:27:47.690 --> 00:27:51.270
from the one that is the one
that generated the observations

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:55.130
is actually a nice
thing to have.

00:27:55.130 --> 00:27:58.770
Kind of a second reason
is that so far, so

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:02.570
remember our generalized
policy iteration scheme,

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:06.690
we described these alternating
processes where we are gradually

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:08.790
improving our policy over time.

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:12.910
And so if you see this now a
little bit more holistically

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.790
in the environment,
we are generating data

00:28:15.790 --> 00:28:22.230
according to changing and
hopefully sequentially improving

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:24.130
policies, versions
of the same policy.

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:27.230
And so far, we are
basically saying

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:31.070
that we have some interactions
with the environment,

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:35.110
and we only use the most recent
versions of those interactions

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:36.490
to update our values.

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:39.590
So all of the previous
data that was generated

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:41.730
from the previous
versions of my policy

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:43.550
we were basically
throwing it away.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:47.990
And so I hope it's
pretty evident

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:50.333
that this might be wasteful.

00:28:50.333 --> 00:28:51.750
So maybe there is
some information

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:53.850
to be gained from previous data.

00:28:57.310 --> 00:29:01.130
And lastly, so this is
slightly more subtle point,

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:04.590
but also I think the reason
why off-policy methods are also

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:10.640
best known for, and it
is because by decoupling,

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.700
if you wish the behavior
policy from the target policy,

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:16.940
we're essentially able to
posit the problem where

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:20.340
we want to learn about
the optimal policy that

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:24.900
is likely to be an
exploiting policy,

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:29.260
typically deterministic from
the exploratory policy which

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:31.420
is the one that is maybe
a little bit more random

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:33.840
and free of exploring
our environment.

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:40.740
So I think these are all great
reasons for which off policy

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:43.800
learning is actually
very important.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:51.020
So now let's look back at what
we were doing for Sarsa where

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:55.740
we were basically updating our
Q functions in an on-policy way.

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:58.460
And let's see how
we could modify

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:01.380
what we're doing in Sarsa
in order to basically follow

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:04.860
this off policy paradigm.

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:08.900
So remember that in Sarsa we
were using behavior policy, mu.

00:30:08.900 --> 00:30:11.280
So here we're already
using the new notation

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:15.380
to generate our
quintuple of events.

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:18.560
Specifically, the
state and control

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:22.640
at time t, the reward and the
next state, and then we're

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:27.040
also using it to generate
the next control.

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:31.240
However, now let's consider an
alternative successor action,

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:35.000
ut plus 1 prime, which is
generated from our target

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.440
policy.

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:40.040
If we were to use now
this quintuple of events

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:43.200
in defining the update,
this was essentially

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:47.280
represent a way in
which we are updating

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:52.640
our Q function, Q, towards the
value of the alternative action.

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:54.520
So if you look a
little bit more closely

00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:58.240
at what we're saying here,
is that we're updating our Q

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:05.420
function for xt, ut by taking a
step in the direction of the TD

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:11.140
target defined by the value
of the alternate action,

00:31:11.140 --> 00:31:13.240
ut prime, t plus 1 prime.

00:31:20.146 --> 00:31:23.240
Now, basically, the
formalization of this,

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:26.620
so how do we-- if we
want to now instantiate

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:29.520
this idea into a
working algorithm,

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:33.560
this would directly lead us
into defining Q-learning.

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:37.060
So specifically,
in Q-learning, we

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:42.100
would define the target policy,
pi, as the greedy policy

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:45.180
with respect to Q.
Again, the target policy

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:47.740
is the policy we wish to learn.

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:50.040
And we are interested in
learning the optimal policy.

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:53.460
So we want to learn the greedy
policy with respect to Q.

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:58.580
So the target policy for
us is the argmax over Q.

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:02.240
And we define the
behavior policy to be

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:04.440
the epsilon greedy policy.

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:08.520
So in this case, we are
acting in some cases

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:11.240
randomly with respect
to our Q function.

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:13.920
But you see how we are
formally defining these two

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.520
different policies,
the one that we

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:18.240
use to interact with the
environment and the one we

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:20.300
actually care about learning.

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:30.920
So by basically looking at
the TD target in this case,

00:32:30.920 --> 00:32:33.820
so remember that in
the previous slide,

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:35.760
we basically said that
in this formalism where

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:37.360
we have a behavior
policy and a target

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:41.240
policy, the update and
specifically the TD target

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:45.480
essentially looks at the reward
plus the discounted value

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:49.120
Q function evaluated in
the alternative action,

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:51.640
ut plus 1 prime.

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:55.840
So if we just take this same
TD target, which is basically

00:32:55.840 --> 00:33:01.340
this term over here, and apply
our definition of how we compute

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:05.460
the alternative actions
according to our target policy,

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:10.900
we only would modify this
term inside the Q function,

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:15.860
namely the argmax
over our Q function.

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:19.600
And by just doing some
algebraic manipulations,

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:21.980
we would basically
get that this is

00:33:21.980 --> 00:33:26.100
equivalent to defining
TD target as the reward

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:30.540
plus the discounted
maximum Q value

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:35.940
where the maximum is
taken under the action, u.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:40.780
So here we're
evaluating the Q value

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:44.100
under the argmax where
the control is taken

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:46.660
under the argmax over
Q. And so this means

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:50.780
that the overall value will be
maximized because we're taking u

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:53.980
in order to maximize Q. And
so this can just be rewritten

00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:58.920
as the max over Q.
All of this to say

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:03.720
is that by following this
formalism, we basically redefine

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:09.199
our TD target in
this way over here,

00:34:09.199 --> 00:34:15.159
meaning we're just replacing
our TD target by defining it

00:34:15.159 --> 00:34:20.020
according to the alternative
successor action, in this case,

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:24.600
the alternative successor
action is the argmax policy.

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:27.280
And so this is what it
leads to, essentially.

00:34:32.199 --> 00:34:33.540
Any questions here?

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:44.520
And so basically, now
if you put this together

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.360
into a working algorithm,
you would basically

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:50.920
get what is referred to
as Q-learning, exactly.

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:55.320
So again, we
initialize our policy.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:58.700
We start our learning
loop whereby we are

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:01.780
initialized in some state, xt.

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:07.520
We basically choose our
control, ut, in this state xt

00:35:07.520 --> 00:35:09.760
according to an
epsilon greedy policy,

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:13.540
this is our behavior
policy, which we chose.

00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:17.060
And we observe the
reward and the next state

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:20.180
according to this control.

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:22.380
Now, we basically have
all of our elements

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:24.780
in order to define
the update which we

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:26.860
derived in the previous slide.

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:32.380
And so we can just repeat
over time until we converge.

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:35.056
Yes.

00:35:35.056 --> 00:35:41.192
The behavior policy only
generates the first-- oh, sorry.

00:35:41.192 --> 00:35:45.280
Behavior policy generates the
first four, right, of the--

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:46.540
OK.

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:48.420
Oh, yes, yes.

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:50.180
Just to make--

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:52.860
Yeah, so in this case, we
are using the behavior policy

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:56.700
in our environment, specifically
this generates the first four.

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:59.440
And then we are kind of
fictitiously assuming

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:01.860
that we would take
this action over here.

00:36:04.840 --> 00:36:05.520
Yeah.

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:08.920
Sorry, what does
this new part-- what

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:13.000
does this new part [INAUDIBLE]?

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:16.300
So here we're defining
two different policies.

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:17.840
One is our behavioral
policy which

00:36:17.840 --> 00:36:19.380
interacts with the environment.

00:36:19.380 --> 00:36:21.760
And one is a target
policy which is

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:24.020
the one we care about
learning, essentially.

00:36:24.020 --> 00:36:27.240
So in this case,
this ut plus 1 prime

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:30.320
is sampled from
this target policy.

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:33.200
So here this is
in general terms.

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:36.560
So we are arbitrarily talking
about behavioral policy mu

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:38.680
and target policy pi.

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:41.880
But in specifically when
we look at Q-learning,

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:47.200
the target policy is the greedy
policy, so taking the argmax.

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:49.860
And the behavior policy is
the epsilon greedy policy.

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:52.600
So we randomly act--

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:55.240
we react randomly epsilon
percent of the time.

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:01.100
So putting this
together, we indeed

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:03.920
follow the epsilon greedy
policy in our environment.

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:08.420
But then in our updates,
we are basically

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:12.660
updating our Q functions towards
the value of the successor

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:15.340
action.

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:16.720
Does this clarify?

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:25.780
So there is some,
of course, there's

00:37:25.780 --> 00:37:28.400
theory that have been developed
for all of these algorithms,

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:30.460
Sarsa included.

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:32.540
I thought it would have
been nice to just mention

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:38.620
the fact that it can be proven
that under some regularity

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:41.260
assumptions, Q-learning
does, indeed,

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:44.220
converge to the optimal
action value function.

00:37:44.220 --> 00:37:46.700
And therefore, this
process essentially

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:49.280
leads to learning the
optimal policy accordingly.

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:58.440
So I think that basically
these differences

00:37:58.440 --> 00:38:02.120
and these discussions that we're
doing between behavior policies

00:38:02.120 --> 00:38:06.500
and target policies can be a
little bit confusing at first.

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:12.160
So let's try to build
some intuition by looking

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:15.440
at the difference in a simple
environment between what we did

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:21.000
before with Sarsa and what we're
doing right now with Q-learning.

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:25.160
So let's look at this modified
version of the gridworld,

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:28.320
whereby again, we want to
go from a start position

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:30.840
to a goal position.

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:33.180
No wind in this case,
so no disturbance.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:35.880
But now we are assuming
we have this, basically

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:39.100
failure region, which
is the cliff over here,

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:41.200
which is basically
something extremely

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:42.500
negative for our agent.

00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:46.040
So the reward here is, again,
encoding minimal time, so

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:48.060
negative 1 at all time steps.

00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:52.740
But if you do end up falling in
the cliff, then you're doomed.

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:56.940
So negative 100, specifically,
and with the specific choice

00:38:56.940 --> 00:38:59.540
of hyperparameters.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:04.220
So as a spoiler, if
you wish, here we

00:39:04.220 --> 00:39:08.780
are comparing the
policies that we

00:39:08.780 --> 00:39:12.620
would be converging
if we followed Sarsa

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:16.560
in blue or Q-learning in red.

00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:25.660
However, if you look at the kind
of training dynamics over here,

00:39:25.660 --> 00:39:28.940
you would basically, again, here
we're looking at on the x-axis

00:39:28.940 --> 00:39:32.020
the episodes, and
on the y-axis we're

00:39:32.020 --> 00:39:34.060
now looking at
the sum of rewards

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:37.180
that we accrue over episodes.

00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:38.780
And from here,
you would actually

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:41.260
say that Sarsa is
indeed performing better

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:43.460
than Q-learning.

00:39:43.460 --> 00:39:49.240
So the catch here is
that if you think back

00:39:49.240 --> 00:39:52.320
at our discussion on-policy
and off-policy algorithms,

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:55.080
Sarsa being an
on-policy algorithm

00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:58.720
is essentially learning
the best version

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:03.420
of its kind of exploratory
policy, if you wish.

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:07.320
So Sarsa, as we defined
it, we were following

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:09.040
this epsilon greedy policy.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:12.120
And so Sarsa indeed converges
to the optimal epsilon

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:14.880
greedy policy.

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:17.440
On the other hand,
Q-learning, because we

00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:19.360
are kind of detaching
our behavior

00:40:19.360 --> 00:40:24.640
from the target of learning
converges to the optimal policy,

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:26.280
pi star.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:30.560
So this is, if you wish, by
induction a property of one

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:34.440
being on-policy and
one being off-policy.

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:38.880
Now, why would this explain
this behavior over here?

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:42.220
The reason is that
in our environment,

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:46.020
remember that both algorithms
act epsilon greedily.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:52.860
So if you act according
to the read policy

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:55.420
and you're epsilon
greedy, then sometimes you

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:57.340
will actually be doing
some random things

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:59.020
and fall in the cliff.

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:02.540
And so this is why
Q-learning when

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:07.820
we log what we're doing over
training, we have a lower score.

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:10.420
On the other hand,
Sarsa, because in order

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:12.760
to account for this
uncertainty of epsilon

00:41:12.760 --> 00:41:16.203
greediness kind of converge
to the optimal epsilon

00:41:16.203 --> 00:41:17.620
greedy policy,
which in this case,

00:41:17.620 --> 00:41:21.220
is a safer one that
basically will travel more

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:24.020
closer to the ceiling.

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.180
Is it generally the
case that Sarsa will

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:30.000
converge to a safer policy?

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.040
So I would say this is
specific, of course,

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:36.060
to this specific choice
of hyperparameters

00:41:36.060 --> 00:41:38.140
and how we are discussing it.

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:42.160
In general, of course, you can
modify the algorithm of Sarsa,

00:41:42.160 --> 00:41:45.520
for example, by having a
decay schedule of your epsilon

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:51.120
greediness and then you can also
converge to the optimal policy.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:53.480
So I mentioned
theory for Q-learning

00:41:53.480 --> 00:41:55.700
that this converges
to the optimal policy.

00:41:55.700 --> 00:41:59.080
There is theory that developed
also for Sarsa in the fact

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:03.280
that according to indeed
some specific choice

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:09.240
of your epsilon decay
strategy or more generally

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:12.640
some specific property of
your randomness in your policy

00:42:12.640 --> 00:42:14.760
and some conditions
on your step size,

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:17.900
Sarsa also converges
to the optimal policy.

00:42:17.900 --> 00:42:18.400
Yeah.

00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:26.320
But why does [INAUDIBLE]
achieve really

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:32.020
converge on lower reward
despite taking a shorter path?

00:42:32.020 --> 00:42:33.680
And why does the--

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:35.820
I mean, both are not
passing through the cliffs.

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:38.320
So why does [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:41.320
No, I think it's worth looking
at this closely and making

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:43.180
sure we're all on the same page.

00:42:43.180 --> 00:42:47.260
So here we're just
visually depicting

00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:53.300
the optimal strategies that
both algorithms converge to.

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:55.020
So we learn some Q functions.

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:57.960
And if we act greedily with
respect to those Q functions,

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:00.620
this is how the two
algorithms would behave.

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:03.460
Now, at training time,
we're not acting greedily

00:43:03.460 --> 00:43:05.280
with respect to
those Q functions,

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:07.880
but we're acting
epsilon greedily.

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:14.140
So with some epsilon percent,
we're doing some random action.

00:43:14.140 --> 00:43:19.140
If you consider the fact
that Q-learning is actually

00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:22.500
going closer to the
cliff, in some epsilon

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:25.380
percent of the time, we would
be landing in the cliff.

00:43:25.380 --> 00:43:30.820
And so this is giving it some
negative reward during training.

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:35.940
So these logs are measuring
the performance of the epsilon

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:41.240
greedy application of the 2Q
functions that we're learning.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:45.160
Also, is the reward negative
one the first step taken?

00:43:45.160 --> 00:43:49.560
Or it's just the
rewarding [INAUDIBLE]?

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:51.340
You mean what is
being tracked here?

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:54.280
That reward negative one.

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:56.320
Here we are assuming
that for every time

00:43:56.320 --> 00:43:59.080
step in our environment,
whatever we do, it's minus 1

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:00.680
until we reach the goal.

00:44:00.680 --> 00:44:03.800
And then if we fall in the
cliff, we get negative 100.

00:44:03.800 --> 00:44:07.400
And it terminates and we are
spawned back to the start.

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:10.440
So even though
during the training

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:13.120
the sum of the rewards
and the episodes

00:44:13.120 --> 00:44:21.320
is lower for Q-learning, because
the more often your [INAUDIBLE],

00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:26.080
the actual fact that
it converges to is,

00:44:26.080 --> 00:44:29.692
I guess, higher in reward
than the blue curve.

00:44:29.692 --> 00:44:30.900
Yeah, this is very important.

00:44:30.900 --> 00:44:31.960
Does it make sense why?

00:44:31.960 --> 00:44:35.840
These two paths
are visualizing--

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:37.720
forget what happens
during training.

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:40.260
Training is done, and we
converge to two different Q

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:41.580
functions.

00:44:41.580 --> 00:44:45.580
This path is visualizing
being greedy, so argmax

00:44:45.580 --> 00:44:47.040
over those Q functions.

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:49.780
And this is what you would do.

00:44:49.780 --> 00:44:55.320
Now, here what we're saying
is during training time,

00:44:55.320 --> 00:44:57.940
so before doing this
visualization, what

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:02.443
you would see is this
basically this plot over here.

00:45:02.443 --> 00:45:03.860
Why would you see
this difference?

00:45:03.860 --> 00:45:05.318
Is because at
training time, you're

00:45:05.318 --> 00:45:08.480
not acting greedily with
respect to your Q function,

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:10.200
but you're acting
epsilon greedily.

00:45:18.660 --> 00:45:20.140
Oh, sorry, yeah.

00:45:20.140 --> 00:45:24.460
Do you do the epsilon
decay [INAUDIBLE]?

00:45:24.460 --> 00:45:27.740
No, so here is just
fixed hyperparameters.

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:29.888
And if you did have [INAUDIBLE]?

00:45:32.700 --> 00:45:35.560
Yeah, so Sarsa would converge
to the optimal policy as well.

00:45:35.560 --> 00:45:36.440
OK.

00:45:36.440 --> 00:45:36.940
Yeah.

00:45:44.440 --> 00:45:49.720
OK, any other questions on what
we discussed so far before we

00:45:49.720 --> 00:45:51.860
move on to function
approximation?

00:45:55.560 --> 00:45:57.680
OK, great.

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:02.720
So until now, we
discussed algorithms

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:05.240
that were basically
deployed on these smaller

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:09.520
scale, toy-like worlds.

00:46:09.520 --> 00:46:14.440
On the other hand, I'm sure most
of you or some of you, at least,

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:18.040
might have heard about the
great successes of reinforcement

00:46:18.040 --> 00:46:20.500
training both in
the realm of games,

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:24.120
for example, AlphaGo
and the successes in Go,

00:46:24.120 --> 00:46:27.080
one of the peaks of the
successes, at least with respect

00:46:27.080 --> 00:46:29.900
to games.

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:31.400
But these are all
environments where

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:34.040
if you think about
the state space,

00:46:34.040 --> 00:46:38.780
these are incredibly large and
incredibly high dimensional.

00:46:38.780 --> 00:46:40.360
So, for example,
in the game of Go,

00:46:40.360 --> 00:46:44.300
you could have 10 to the power
of 170 different configurations

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:46.060
of the board, which
is, I think, more

00:46:46.060 --> 00:46:48.320
than the atoms in the universe
or something like that.

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:51.180
So this is an
incredibly high number.

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:55.540
And you could argue that games
are actually among the easier

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:59.300
things to solve compared
to having things deployed

00:46:59.300 --> 00:47:01.820
in the real world where
things that could happen

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:04.920
are much more high
dimensional, even.

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:09.260
And so the question
is, how can we

00:47:09.260 --> 00:47:15.180
scale what we're saying with
this model free reinforcement

00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:21.100
learning approaches to these
more high dimensional systems?

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:24.740
And the culprit here in
scaling will essentially

00:47:24.740 --> 00:47:32.580
be to move away from our tabular
expression of value functions.

00:47:32.580 --> 00:47:36.080
Again, remember that we are
essentially in everything

00:47:36.080 --> 00:47:38.120
that we did so far,
we were assuming

00:47:38.120 --> 00:47:44.960
that the value functions
or Q functions equally

00:47:44.960 --> 00:47:48.880
were essentially represented
as lookup tables.

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:53.120
So essentially, for each value
of our state or state action

00:47:53.120 --> 00:47:55.040
pair, we would have
a specific entry

00:47:55.040 --> 00:48:00.560
which represents the value of
that state or state action pair.

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:04.860
On the other hand, and this
is, of course, problematic,

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:07.820
as I'm sure you can imagine,
for two main reasons.

00:48:07.820 --> 00:48:09.040
The first one is memory.

00:48:09.040 --> 00:48:13.240
So if you have 10 to
the power of 170 states,

00:48:13.240 --> 00:48:16.600
then even just storing
this is an issue.

00:48:16.600 --> 00:48:18.460
But then from the
perspective of learning,

00:48:18.460 --> 00:48:20.960
there is a clear curse
of dimensionality issue

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:26.740
that comes up because to update
values in our lookup tables,

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:30.640
we have to visit those
entries in the lookup table.

00:48:30.640 --> 00:48:35.910
And so you can imagine how
this becomes problematic.

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:41.470
So this is where value function
approximation comes into play.

00:48:41.470 --> 00:48:44.310
And this is because-- and
basically, where the main idea

00:48:44.310 --> 00:48:47.710
is that rather than defining
these Lookup tables,

00:48:47.710 --> 00:48:50.190
we want to define a
parametric function

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:52.030
with some parameters,
theta, that basically

00:48:52.030 --> 00:48:55.430
approximates our values.

00:48:55.430 --> 00:48:58.790
So we will be moving from
these tabular representations

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:01.830
to something where the
value function is basically

00:49:01.830 --> 00:49:04.670
a parametric function,
parameters theta, that

00:49:04.670 --> 00:49:11.150
in this case, maps from
states to a real value.

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:14.450
And you can imagine something
very similar for the Q function.

00:49:14.450 --> 00:49:25.070
Again, it's just a function
that basically maps from states

00:49:25.070 --> 00:49:26.810
and controls to real values.

00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:37.690
So first of all, how does this
idea address these two issues?

00:49:37.690 --> 00:49:42.490
First of all, we
are compressing,

00:49:42.490 --> 00:49:45.250
or if you wish, amortizing
the memory storage

00:49:45.250 --> 00:49:49.610
cost in storing
only the parameters.

00:49:49.610 --> 00:49:52.630
So we're not storing one value
for each state action pair,

00:49:52.630 --> 00:49:56.530
but we're only storing the
values of our parameters.

00:49:56.530 --> 00:50:00.670
And also in terms of addressing
the curse of dimensionality,

00:50:00.670 --> 00:50:05.450
to some extent, we can hope
that these value approximations

00:50:05.450 --> 00:50:09.410
can generalize across states
and state action pairs.

00:50:09.410 --> 00:50:14.330
So far, even in our
gridworld examples,

00:50:14.330 --> 00:50:22.330
we were assuming that we had
a goal state, g, and that even

00:50:22.330 --> 00:50:27.010
very nearby states,
if we wish to estimate

00:50:27.010 --> 00:50:29.130
the value of this nearby
state to the goal,

00:50:29.130 --> 00:50:31.310
we would have to
visit this state.

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:33.430
On the other hand,
you can probably

00:50:33.430 --> 00:50:36.110
hope that in the
general case, as long

00:50:36.110 --> 00:50:39.150
as your representation of
your environment is correct,

00:50:39.150 --> 00:50:41.950
this kind of knowing
something about the goal state

00:50:41.950 --> 00:50:45.350
could also inform your
estimate of the value close

00:50:45.350 --> 00:50:46.870
by the goal state.

00:50:46.870 --> 00:50:49.270
And so this is
something that we can

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:52.730
hope to have when we look
at function approximation.

00:50:59.750 --> 00:51:02.510
How does function
approximation look in practice

00:51:02.510 --> 00:51:05.310
for value functions?

00:51:05.310 --> 00:51:08.790
Here is where there could
be many different ways

00:51:08.790 --> 00:51:13.130
of instantiating value functions
through function approximation.

00:51:13.130 --> 00:51:15.990
At the high level, I
think most implementations

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:20.110
pick between one of these
three representations.

00:51:20.110 --> 00:51:23.430
In general, we are assuming
that we have a black box that

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:26.270
basically represents our
function approximation

00:51:26.270 --> 00:51:28.690
with some parameters, theta.

00:51:28.690 --> 00:51:31.350
But, in general, we could
have these three main classes.

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:34.770
The first one is if you're
estimating value state value

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:36.930
functions, v, it
will be something

00:51:36.930 --> 00:51:40.970
that takes as input as state
x, does some computation,

00:51:40.970 --> 00:51:44.450
and then spits out a
value for that state.

00:51:44.450 --> 00:51:47.930
Similarly, the Q function
could be something

00:51:47.930 --> 00:51:49.950
that takes as input
a state and a control

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:52.570
and spits out the specific
Q function for that state

00:51:52.570 --> 00:51:54.490
and control pair.

00:51:54.490 --> 00:52:00.170
Or another interpretation
of this second approach

00:52:00.170 --> 00:52:04.570
would be something that we still
want to estimate the Q function.

00:52:04.570 --> 00:52:06.990
But now we take as
input only the state.

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:09.230
And rather than
generating one output,

00:52:09.230 --> 00:52:13.090
we would be generating
multiple outputs one

00:52:13.090 --> 00:52:15.670
for each possible
control in that state.

00:52:19.090 --> 00:52:20.690
Can you imagine why--

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:22.470
I'm not saying this
is better in general,

00:52:22.470 --> 00:52:24.750
but can you imagine why
this might be desirable

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:26.710
compared to the second one?

00:52:26.710 --> 00:52:27.610
Yeah.

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:34.950
[INAUDIBLE] in your function,
or maybe even you put inputs,

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:38.230
then you could
define the function

00:52:38.230 --> 00:52:42.910
in terms of new [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:42.910 --> 00:52:45.930
So I think your
intuition is correct.

00:52:45.930 --> 00:52:49.670
Now doing this for
continuous action spaces

00:52:49.670 --> 00:52:52.770
is challenging depending
on discretization.

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:55.430
But I would not go for this when
you have a continuous control

00:52:55.430 --> 00:52:56.365
space.

00:52:56.365 --> 00:52:57.990
But I think you're
correct in the sense

00:52:57.990 --> 00:53:01.390
that this could lead to
some simplification in how

00:53:01.390 --> 00:53:04.990
you represent Q. But can you--

00:53:04.990 --> 00:53:10.470
so assume both x and u
are discrete in our case.

00:53:10.470 --> 00:53:12.730
Can you think of a reason why?

00:53:12.730 --> 00:53:13.670
Yeah.

00:53:13.670 --> 00:53:15.650
[INAUDIBLE] you
might want to have

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:17.790
multiple [INAUDIBLE] functions.

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:20.670
Like if you were to
drive your car somewhere

00:53:20.670 --> 00:53:24.790
where distance in car versus
cost of gas [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:27.410 --> 00:53:30.550
I would argue probably not.

00:53:30.550 --> 00:53:31.990
No, at least at face value.

00:53:31.990 --> 00:53:34.050
Because remember that
we will be posting

00:53:34.050 --> 00:53:36.970
this as a regression problem.

00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:43.222
So either here you are assuming
that you have different target Q

00:53:43.222 --> 00:53:45.430
functions that you're willing
to wanting to estimate,

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:48.330
and then maybe indeed
this could be helpful.

00:53:48.330 --> 00:53:50.730
But let's assume
for now that we have

00:53:50.730 --> 00:53:54.247
one value function of one
policy that we wish to estimate.

00:53:54.247 --> 00:53:56.330
But I think yours is a
good intuition but not what

00:53:56.330 --> 00:53:58.930
I was looking here right now.

00:53:58.930 --> 00:54:02.890
I mean, what's this probe
one specific state and then

00:54:02.890 --> 00:54:06.770
we can take the argmax over all
the Qs that output to get the--

00:54:06.770 --> 00:54:08.030
Yes, exactly, exactly.

00:54:08.030 --> 00:54:10.230
So I think these are
all great points.

00:54:10.230 --> 00:54:12.330
But here I was more
thinking about, indeed,

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:15.050
more of a computational
aspect, if you wish.

00:54:15.050 --> 00:54:19.390
So remember that we are
defining our policy generally,

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:22.390
or at least a greedy policy once
we have learned the Q function

00:54:22.390 --> 00:54:24.830
as an argmax over u.

00:54:24.830 --> 00:54:28.170
Now, say that we have,
again, u is discrete,

00:54:28.170 --> 00:54:31.750
so we have a finite number
of u we want to iterate over.

00:54:31.750 --> 00:54:37.870
If we had now a function
approximation for our Q

00:54:37.870 --> 00:54:40.990
function, this becomes
an optimization problem,

00:54:40.990 --> 00:54:41.730
if you wish.

00:54:41.730 --> 00:54:45.370
So before it was just basically
looking up in a table.

00:54:45.370 --> 00:54:47.350
Now, it's an
optimization problem.

00:54:47.350 --> 00:54:52.070
So if our Q function
is something that given

00:54:52.070 --> 00:54:55.310
a state only gives
us the value of--

00:54:55.310 --> 00:54:57.670
given a state and a
control gives us one value,

00:54:57.670 --> 00:55:03.030
we would have to do n queries
where n could be very high.

00:55:03.030 --> 00:55:06.670
On the other hand, if Q
is an object that given

00:55:06.670 --> 00:55:12.270
an x gives us all the values
of u of qxu for all u,

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:15.150
then this becomes again
a kind of a lookup

00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:18.050
in a finite number of
elements, if that makes sense,

00:55:18.050 --> 00:55:19.330
with only one call.

00:55:23.050 --> 00:55:25.710
Again, I'm not saying this is
the best way for going for it,

00:55:25.710 --> 00:55:32.190
but these are kind of
insights behind these choices.

00:55:36.090 --> 00:55:38.370
And then not only
is there, of course,

00:55:38.370 --> 00:55:41.770
flexibility in how you define
these functional approximations

00:55:41.770 --> 00:55:48.090
at a high level, but also
within the Black box creativity

00:55:48.090 --> 00:55:49.910
is king here.

00:55:49.910 --> 00:55:53.430
So in some sense, you could
go with something very simple,

00:55:53.430 --> 00:55:55.670
such as a linear model or
something very complex,

00:55:55.670 --> 00:55:58.170
such as an extremely
deep neural network.

00:55:58.170 --> 00:56:02.270
But the high level
understanding remains the same.

00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:09.770
So I hope I have convinced you
about the benefits of value

00:56:09.770 --> 00:56:11.530
functions approximations.

00:56:11.530 --> 00:56:16.770
How do we instantiate what we've
been saying so far in terms

00:56:16.770 --> 00:56:19.870
of Monte Carlo learning
and TD learning

00:56:19.870 --> 00:56:22.110
when we have value
functions where

00:56:22.110 --> 00:56:24.950
we have parametric
functions that

00:56:24.950 --> 00:56:29.070
represent our value functions?

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:31.830
So remember that now
what we want to do

00:56:31.830 --> 00:56:37.350
is essentially learn the
parameters, theta, of our value

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:38.650
function approximators.

00:56:38.650 --> 00:56:42.750
That is, we want to minimize
the error between the estimated

00:56:42.750 --> 00:56:47.350
value, for example,
v theta of x.

00:56:47.350 --> 00:56:50.190
So the error we want to minimize
this error between v theta of x

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:54.990
and the true value v pi of x.

00:56:54.990 --> 00:56:57.370
Seeing this from a
regression perspective,

00:56:57.370 --> 00:56:59.350
this would mean
potentially positing

00:56:59.350 --> 00:57:03.750
this objective function,
j, or cost function j.

00:57:03.750 --> 00:57:08.390
And we want to minimize the
expected value of this error.

00:57:08.390 --> 00:57:11.610
So we want to minimize the mean
squared error, for example.

00:57:14.495 --> 00:57:15.370
How do we solve this?

00:57:15.370 --> 00:57:17.570
We have done this multiple
times, for example,

00:57:17.570 --> 00:57:20.170
by taking a gradient descent.

00:57:20.170 --> 00:57:28.750
So where we want to define
essentially some delta,

00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:31.370
some displacement in our
parameters of our function

00:57:31.370 --> 00:57:34.250
approximators, which
goes in the direction

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:36.850
of the negative gradient.

00:57:36.850 --> 00:57:43.410
Now, if we were to pick
this choice of displacement,

00:57:43.410 --> 00:57:46.010
delta t, what we
would basically get

00:57:46.010 --> 00:57:49.210
doing some math is
something such as we

00:57:49.210 --> 00:57:51.490
are basically taking
one step where

00:57:51.490 --> 00:57:58.810
we choose some step size, alpha,
in the direction of basically

00:57:58.810 --> 00:58:06.030
our negative gradient multiplied
by this error over here.

00:58:06.030 --> 00:58:08.370
And I definitely suggest
you to basically go

00:58:08.370 --> 00:58:11.150
through the derivation of
how we go from here to here.

00:58:11.150 --> 00:58:13.270
But this is not very insightful.

00:58:13.270 --> 00:58:15.250
It's just basically
applying gradient descent,

00:58:15.250 --> 00:58:18.630
stochastic gradient
descent to this objective.

00:58:18.630 --> 00:58:21.070
But at a high level, it's
important to basically fix

00:58:21.070 --> 00:58:25.110
in your mind that the form of
our updates in our parameters

00:58:25.110 --> 00:58:26.690
is something that
looks like this.

00:58:31.550 --> 00:58:33.310
Now, how we described
it so far, it's

00:58:33.310 --> 00:58:35.790
kind of a chicken and
egg in an egg problem

00:58:35.790 --> 00:58:37.990
because we're saying
we want to minimize

00:58:37.990 --> 00:58:42.110
the error between our prediction
and our true value function,

00:58:42.110 --> 00:58:44.370
but we don't have the
true value function.

00:58:44.370 --> 00:58:49.870
And so in practice,
what we do here

00:58:49.870 --> 00:58:51.750
is we basically
leverage everything

00:58:51.750 --> 00:58:56.230
that we said so far when
we talked about TD targets

00:58:56.230 --> 00:58:59.350
and Monte Carlo targets
in order to define

00:58:59.350 --> 00:59:02.550
what is our target for
regression, essentially.

00:59:02.550 --> 00:59:06.170
So similarly to how we
did in tabular methods,

00:59:06.170 --> 00:59:09.390
we would have a version
of the Monte Carlo update

00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:11.750
where now we are
taking we're updating

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:15.370
the parameters of our function
in the direction of minimizing

00:59:15.370 --> 00:59:18.650
the prediction error or
this prediction error is

00:59:18.650 --> 00:59:21.210
between our prediction
and the ground truth

00:59:21.210 --> 00:59:26.250
return estimated as we were
doing through Monte Carlo.

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:29.510
And in TD learning, what we
would get is the same thing.

00:59:29.510 --> 00:59:34.290
But now, instead of pointing
at the observed return,

00:59:34.290 --> 00:59:37.810
we're basically pointing
at the TD target

00:59:37.810 --> 00:59:41.310
where we're using our estimate
of future values as well.

00:59:48.810 --> 00:59:52.130
OK, so what are we
really saying here?

00:59:52.130 --> 00:59:58.170
So we are basically saying
two different versions

00:59:58.170 --> 01:00:00.830
of a very similar intuition.

01:00:00.830 --> 01:00:03.970
So we said in the
tabular dimension

01:00:03.970 --> 01:00:07.450
where value functions
are lookup tables,

01:00:07.450 --> 01:00:10.210
we were using interactions
with our environment

01:00:10.210 --> 01:00:15.670
to estimate either our
return, simply the sum

01:00:15.670 --> 01:00:19.570
of all the rewards that
we saw or a TD target.

01:00:19.570 --> 01:00:25.590
So the immediate reward plus
the estimated future value,

01:00:25.590 --> 01:00:30.630
in the tabular case, we were
then simply using these two

01:00:30.630 --> 01:00:35.830
quantities to update individual
entries in our lookup table

01:00:35.830 --> 01:00:39.210
according to our
update equations.

01:00:42.910 --> 01:00:45.030
When we're looking at
function approximation,

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:47.950
at the high level, we're
doing exactly the same thing.

01:00:47.950 --> 01:00:51.730
But now, instead of updating
entries in lookup tables,

01:00:51.730 --> 01:00:54.310
we're using those
same quantities.

01:00:54.310 --> 01:00:59.590
The return, gt,
and the TD target,

01:00:59.590 --> 01:01:06.150
to basically define input
output pairs for our regression

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:07.870
problem.

01:01:07.870 --> 01:01:10.290
And so this means
that we will be

01:01:10.290 --> 01:01:14.130
able to define some updates
to our parameters, theta,

01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:16.690
in a very similar way.

01:01:16.690 --> 01:01:19.290
Does this make sense?

01:01:19.290 --> 01:01:20.350
Any questions here?

01:01:31.510 --> 01:01:32.402
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:41.810 --> 01:01:47.370
Yes, so this is a great
intuition because indeed,

01:01:47.370 --> 01:01:52.150
as you say, the quality of
my target evolves over time

01:01:52.150 --> 01:01:53.710
the more this is getting better.

01:01:53.710 --> 01:01:56.970
And so you are
actually pointing at,

01:01:56.970 --> 01:02:01.590
I would say, one of the key
engineering breakthroughs,

01:02:01.590 --> 01:02:04.130
if you wish, that enabled
deep reinforcement

01:02:04.130 --> 01:02:06.450
learning to actually
work in practice,

01:02:06.450 --> 01:02:10.590
basically stabilizing learning
exactly for this reason.

01:02:10.590 --> 01:02:13.110
And I think we'll mention
it in a couple of slides.

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:14.610
Yeah, but this is
a great intuition.

01:02:23.510 --> 01:02:27.590
Of course, again, here
we mentioned everything

01:02:27.590 --> 01:02:31.070
in looking at state
value functions.

01:02:31.070 --> 01:02:33.190
But everything holds
exactly the same

01:02:33.190 --> 01:02:35.350
when we look at Q functions.

01:02:35.350 --> 01:02:38.630
So again, the underlying
regression problem

01:02:38.630 --> 01:02:41.030
that we would like
to solve is basically

01:02:41.030 --> 01:02:45.710
minimizing the error between
the predicted Q function

01:02:45.710 --> 01:02:49.030
and the ground truth
Q function, which

01:02:49.030 --> 01:02:52.950
leads to defining a specific
update in parameter space.

01:02:52.950 --> 01:02:56.950
We are, of course, not able
to observe the ground truth

01:02:56.950 --> 01:03:02.150
Q function, and so we define
the target of our regression

01:03:02.150 --> 01:03:04.950
either through Monte
Carlo or TD learning.

01:03:04.950 --> 01:03:07.830
In this case, if we
were to use a TD target,

01:03:07.830 --> 01:03:11.450
we would get what is referred
to in literature as fitted Q

01:03:11.450 --> 01:03:12.270
iteration.

01:03:16.690 --> 01:03:21.650
And so, indeed,
implementing this

01:03:21.650 --> 01:03:27.410
in within our generalized
policy iteration scheme

01:03:27.410 --> 01:03:31.570
could look like something where
at the policy evaluation stage,

01:03:31.570 --> 01:03:35.970
we basically approximate
the Q function of our policy

01:03:35.970 --> 01:03:39.530
by using these update
equations in parameter space

01:03:39.530 --> 01:03:40.770
that we just defined.

01:03:40.770 --> 01:03:44.490
Namely, we interact with the
environment, collect some data,

01:03:44.490 --> 01:03:47.530
and fit that data, fit
the Q function that better

01:03:47.530 --> 01:03:49.370
estimates those TD targets.

01:03:49.370 --> 01:03:52.650
And so this is what policy
evaluation looks like for us.

01:03:52.650 --> 01:03:56.210
And then we use the
updated Q function

01:03:56.210 --> 01:04:01.050
to basically define our epsilon
greedy policy improvement step.

01:04:01.050 --> 01:04:06.030
Again, I hope that even
through the repetition

01:04:06.030 --> 01:04:08.530
of very similar versions
of those same algorithms,

01:04:08.530 --> 01:04:11.670
you're starting to see how
different RL algorithms are all

01:04:11.670 --> 01:04:14.950
built around different
instantiations of Monte Carlo

01:04:14.950 --> 01:04:16.790
learning and TD
learning and basically

01:04:16.790 --> 01:04:21.470
swapping or mixing different
elements in those updates.

01:04:21.470 --> 01:04:25.390
But this would, indeed,
be a working reinforcement

01:04:25.390 --> 01:04:29.370
learning algorithm with
value function approximation.

01:04:33.230 --> 01:04:36.790
How does this look like in our
reinforcement learning skeleton?

01:04:36.790 --> 01:04:38.710
Remember that last
time we were starting

01:04:38.710 --> 01:04:42.150
to introduce this bird's
eye view on basically

01:04:42.150 --> 01:04:43.990
all reinforcement
learning algorithms out

01:04:43.990 --> 01:04:47.110
there defined as a
first stage where

01:04:47.110 --> 01:04:50.030
we generate samples, a
second stage where we either

01:04:50.030 --> 01:04:53.230
fit a model of our dynamics
or estimate some return,

01:04:53.230 --> 01:04:55.950
and use this to
improve the policy.

01:04:55.950 --> 01:04:58.270
Indeed, if we look at
fitted Q-learning, which

01:04:58.270 --> 01:05:02.810
is what we just described,
we would run the policy

01:05:02.810 --> 01:05:06.290
and observe our
usual transitions.

01:05:06.290 --> 01:05:09.970
We would then use
this data to define

01:05:09.970 --> 01:05:16.010
a target for our Q function
regression problem, if you wish.

01:05:16.010 --> 01:05:19.410
Specifically, here we're
using the TD target.

01:05:19.410 --> 01:05:23.810
And then use this
to basically update

01:05:23.810 --> 01:05:26.570
our estimate of the Q function.

01:05:26.570 --> 01:05:29.650
And then we use the
newly learned Q function

01:05:29.650 --> 01:05:33.710
to improve the policy according
to an argmax policy improvement.

01:05:38.370 --> 01:05:40.430
Any questions here?

01:05:46.370 --> 01:05:50.010
So then the last thing that
I wanted to discuss today

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:53.190
relates with basically
taking these ideas

01:05:53.190 --> 01:05:57.230
but now looking at modern
instantiations of these ideas,

01:05:57.230 --> 01:05:59.870
specifically in the field
of deep reinforcement

01:05:59.870 --> 01:06:02.590
learning, that is, using
deep neural networks

01:06:02.590 --> 01:06:06.790
within the same algorithms
that we just discussed today.

01:06:06.790 --> 01:06:11.190
And one of, I would say,
groundbreaking results,

01:06:11.190 --> 01:06:14.430
I think this dates
back to roughly 2014,

01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:19.430
is known as Deep Q-Networks
or DQN for short.

01:06:19.430 --> 01:06:22.310
And we saw this,
I think, a couple

01:06:22.310 --> 01:06:25.370
of times before we saw these
videos of these Atari games.

01:06:25.370 --> 01:06:27.530
But the point here
is that, indeed,

01:06:27.530 --> 01:06:30.870
the goal of where these
algorithms were developed

01:06:30.870 --> 01:06:33.230
was solving basically
learn how to play

01:06:33.230 --> 01:06:37.350
optimally different versions
of the Atari family of games.

01:06:37.350 --> 01:06:39.870
Specifically, the
interesting side of this

01:06:39.870 --> 01:06:43.070
is that we're basically using
deep neural network specifically

01:06:43.070 --> 01:06:45.690
at the time, convolutional
neural networks,

01:06:45.690 --> 01:06:49.130
to learn how to control these
games directly from images.

01:06:49.130 --> 01:06:51.290
And so this is where the
power of if you wish,

01:06:51.290 --> 01:06:52.957
deep learning and
reinforcement learning

01:06:52.957 --> 01:06:54.950
was starting to be combined.

01:06:54.950 --> 01:07:00.170
And at a high level, the
architecture of these methods

01:07:00.170 --> 01:07:05.050
looked something like we have
raw images of our environment,

01:07:05.050 --> 01:07:07.890
which we are processing
through multiple layers

01:07:07.890 --> 01:07:10.290
of convolutional
neural networks.

01:07:10.290 --> 01:07:12.570
And then we're
outputting here, at least

01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:17.610
in the original
instantiation, one value

01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:20.850
for each of the possible actions
that we could do in this state.

01:07:20.850 --> 01:07:24.290
So if you wish, the image for
us is a proxy or observation

01:07:24.290 --> 01:07:26.250
of what the state x is.

01:07:26.250 --> 01:07:30.370
And so we have the state coming
in in the form of an image.

01:07:30.370 --> 01:07:33.570
And we have the Q
value for all of the u

01:07:33.570 --> 01:07:35.890
of our controls in this state.

01:07:35.890 --> 01:07:38.530
So if you remember from our
three different categories

01:07:38.530 --> 01:07:42.350
of value function approximation,
this would be the third one.

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:50.290
So in some way, this work,
indeed, used deep neural nets

01:07:50.290 --> 01:07:52.970
to represent our Q functions.

01:07:52.970 --> 01:07:54.870
And as we'll see
in the next slide,

01:07:54.870 --> 01:07:58.870
this introduced a number of
tricks that basically helped

01:07:58.870 --> 01:08:02.250
to stabilize learning when we
look at deep neural networks,

01:08:02.250 --> 01:08:05.290
exactly as your colleague
was assuming before.

01:08:08.030 --> 01:08:09.750
Is this picture
clear, more or less

01:08:09.750 --> 01:08:11.530
what the algorithm is doing?

01:08:15.950 --> 01:08:22.870
So let's look more specifically
at these two solutions that

01:08:22.870 --> 01:08:26.270
were I would say extremely
important for actually

01:08:26.270 --> 01:08:28.470
the success of these methods.

01:08:28.470 --> 01:08:32.390
And we're specifically talking
about experience replay

01:08:32.390 --> 01:08:37.029
and what is known as
using fixed Q targets.

01:08:37.029 --> 01:08:38.790
So indeed, these
two ideas turned out

01:08:38.790 --> 01:08:43.350
to be extremely important
to stabilize training

01:08:43.350 --> 01:08:46.470
because they basically
addressed two main issues.

01:08:46.470 --> 01:08:51.430
The first one is
that right now, if we

01:08:51.430 --> 01:08:55.660
are looking at function
approximation of our Q

01:08:55.660 --> 01:08:58.819
functions, we basically
have this inner loop,

01:08:58.819 --> 01:09:03.540
which is basically
regression of our Q function.

01:09:03.540 --> 01:09:06.020
So as we explore
in our environment,

01:09:06.020 --> 01:09:10.060
we basically are iteratively
fitting the estimates of our Q

01:09:10.060 --> 01:09:13.620
function to the targets
that we are observing.

01:09:13.620 --> 01:09:16.979
However, in this
case, because we

01:09:16.979 --> 01:09:19.160
are active agents
in our environment,

01:09:19.160 --> 01:09:22.819
kind of samples over
time in our rollouts

01:09:22.819 --> 01:09:25.540
are highly correlated
with each other.

01:09:25.540 --> 01:09:31.180
And this is, of course, very
bad from our perspective

01:09:31.180 --> 01:09:34.220
of regression and
supervised learning

01:09:34.220 --> 01:09:38.220
because we know that statistical
learning theory usually

01:09:38.220 --> 01:09:43.260
assumes that data is IID
from a target distribution.

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:46.620
And so the one big issue
is that because we are now

01:09:46.620 --> 01:09:52.540
in an interactive engagement
with our environment,

01:09:52.540 --> 01:09:54.240
the targets and
the samples that we

01:09:54.240 --> 01:09:57.440
observe are highly
correlated with each other.

01:09:57.440 --> 01:10:00.400
The second problem is,
indeed, what your colleague

01:10:00.400 --> 01:10:03.760
was referring to, which is that
the target that we are aiming

01:10:03.760 --> 01:10:06.000
towards that we are
regressing towards to

01:10:06.000 --> 01:10:07.900
is, indeed, a moving target.

01:10:07.900 --> 01:10:10.860
Because as we update
the parameters theta,

01:10:10.860 --> 01:10:13.320
then the target of our
regression also changes.

01:10:13.320 --> 01:10:18.440
And so this creates some
problems in terms of stability.

01:10:18.440 --> 01:10:24.280
So let us look at how these two
issues were solved in practice.

01:10:24.280 --> 01:10:25.920
So at the very
high level, I would

01:10:25.920 --> 01:10:31.280
say the learning loop
of DQN goes as follows.

01:10:31.280 --> 01:10:34.120
So as usual, we are in
a specific state, xt,

01:10:34.120 --> 01:10:36.040
and we take an
action ut according

01:10:36.040 --> 01:10:37.660
to the epsilon greedy policy.

01:10:37.660 --> 01:10:41.400
Again, the policy initially
might be a randomly initialized

01:10:41.400 --> 01:10:43.800
neural net.

01:10:43.800 --> 01:10:47.540
As we are doing
classically in Q-learning,

01:10:47.540 --> 01:10:53.580
we store this transition of
xt, ut reward and next state.

01:10:53.580 --> 01:10:59.020
And we store it in a replay
memory calligraphic t.

01:10:59.020 --> 01:11:03.380
Now, so here is where the
solution to the first problem

01:11:03.380 --> 01:11:06.442
actually comes in place,
which is arguably very simple,

01:11:06.442 --> 01:11:07.900
but it's essentially
saying that we

01:11:07.900 --> 01:11:10.940
know that the problem
that we want to fix

01:11:10.940 --> 01:11:13.620
is that data and
individual transitions

01:11:13.620 --> 01:11:16.380
are highly correlated
between each other.

01:11:16.380 --> 01:11:21.300
So let's decorrelate transitions
by storing them in a buffer.

01:11:21.300 --> 01:11:25.060
And then rather than using only
the most immediate transitions,

01:11:25.060 --> 01:11:28.900
let us subsample these
individual transitions

01:11:28.900 --> 01:11:30.080
from that buffer.

01:11:33.420 --> 01:11:35.960
Assuming the buffer
is large enough,

01:11:35.960 --> 01:11:38.800
then this would-- the
more the buffer grows,

01:11:38.800 --> 01:11:43.180
the more the individual
transitions are decorrelated

01:11:43.180 --> 01:11:45.460
with each other.

01:11:45.460 --> 01:11:49.320
So what we're
saying is that if we

01:11:49.320 --> 01:11:53.960
were to look at
a single rollout,

01:11:53.960 --> 01:11:57.240
we would generally
observe a situation where

01:11:57.240 --> 01:12:01.280
we have xt, ut and
the associated reward,

01:12:01.280 --> 01:12:03.440
and we have the next
state xt plus 1, which

01:12:03.440 --> 01:12:05.660
gives us the first transition.

01:12:13.800 --> 01:12:17.020
And so we could use this to make
one update in our algorithm.

01:12:17.020 --> 01:12:20.560
But then as we move
along in our episode,

01:12:20.560 --> 01:12:29.840
we would basically be
observing some new transitions

01:12:29.840 --> 01:12:32.640
which are highly correlated
with the previous one.

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:37.120
And so if we were to take
these transitions online and

01:12:37.120 --> 01:12:38.680
sequentially, this
would be highly

01:12:38.680 --> 01:12:39.960
correlated with each other.

01:12:39.960 --> 01:12:43.560
What we're saying, at least with
this experience replay idea,

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:46.980
is that rather than using
these transitions directly,

01:12:46.980 --> 01:12:52.420
we observe them, store them in
a buffer, which is something

01:12:52.420 --> 01:12:57.260
that it's just a large data
set of transitions, something

01:12:57.260 --> 01:13:01.440
that is a collection
of these transitions--

01:13:05.260 --> 01:13:08.180
and xt plus 1.

01:13:08.180 --> 01:13:09.820
And by sampling
from this buffer,

01:13:09.820 --> 01:13:12.580
we are basically
decorrelating the transitions

01:13:12.580 --> 01:13:14.640
that we use to fit our networks.

01:13:17.500 --> 01:13:18.980
So again, very
simple idea, but I

01:13:18.980 --> 01:13:21.060
think quite powerful
in accomplishing

01:13:21.060 --> 01:13:22.160
what I was trying to do.

01:13:24.740 --> 01:13:30.740
Now, the second trick entails
stabilizing this moving target

01:13:30.740 --> 01:13:32.980
issue that we were
highlighting before.

01:13:32.980 --> 01:13:36.260
And the idea here is
to basically leverage

01:13:36.260 --> 01:13:40.500
two copies of the same
value function network.

01:13:40.500 --> 01:13:44.680
So instead of having a
single network, Q theta,

01:13:44.680 --> 01:13:47.800
we're basically working with
two different networks, Q theta

01:13:47.800 --> 01:13:50.240
and Q phi.

01:13:50.240 --> 01:13:54.240
So we have two copies
of the parameters.

01:13:54.240 --> 01:14:02.000
The point in this
solution is essentially

01:14:02.000 --> 01:14:09.440
that we will have one
copy of the parameters

01:14:09.440 --> 01:14:13.520
that we are updating over time
and one copy that is basically

01:14:13.520 --> 01:14:18.280
a delayed version of that
function that we're updating.

01:14:18.280 --> 01:14:24.120
So we start with some randomly
initialized to the same thing

01:14:24.120 --> 01:14:28.040
policy, so two copies of the
same initialized network.

01:14:28.040 --> 01:14:31.800
Now, imagine that we have
this is like a time axis

01:14:31.800 --> 01:14:33.400
overtraining.

01:14:33.400 --> 01:14:43.000
We would have a phase where we
are initially updating theta.

01:14:43.000 --> 01:14:47.620
And we are updating
theta on fixed phi.

01:14:47.620 --> 01:14:50.400
So over here phi
is actually fixed.

01:14:53.860 --> 01:14:58.980
Now, phi could be
horribly bad at estimating

01:14:58.980 --> 01:15:00.580
the values at the beginning.

01:15:00.580 --> 01:15:03.340
But from the perspective
of stability of the target,

01:15:03.340 --> 01:15:08.660
this is actually stable because
it's consistent over time.

01:15:08.660 --> 01:15:14.620
Of course, over time it will be
defining some regular cadence

01:15:14.620 --> 01:15:20.180
where we are basically
updating the parameters, phi,

01:15:20.180 --> 01:15:25.380
to the potentially most
recent version of theta.

01:15:25.380 --> 01:15:27.640
And so we repeat this.

01:15:27.640 --> 01:15:31.160
So then we have a new phase
where we are updating theta.

01:15:31.160 --> 01:15:33.380
But now towards a
slightly better version

01:15:33.380 --> 01:15:35.220
of the target network.

01:15:35.220 --> 01:15:37.240
Again, phi here remains fixed.

01:15:40.500 --> 01:15:42.000
And then we repeat over time.

01:15:42.000 --> 01:15:44.040
So you see that, in
general, we basically

01:15:44.040 --> 01:15:48.640
instantiate this delayed version
of the same Q-network which

01:15:48.640 --> 01:15:57.000
stabilizes the target through
this kind of delayed update.

01:15:57.000 --> 01:15:59.680
Of course, different
versions vary

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:03.420
of the algorithm vary in
how this update is done.

01:16:03.420 --> 01:16:10.380
This is a very deterministic way
of updating the target policy,

01:16:10.380 --> 01:16:12.360
Q phi.

01:16:12.360 --> 01:16:17.040
It's been shown that this
specific version is probably

01:16:17.040 --> 01:16:19.320
not the best choice
because there could

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:22.760
be a jump, a too
harsh jump in updating

01:16:22.760 --> 01:16:24.680
the parameters of
our target phi.

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:26.360
So I would say
probably right now

01:16:26.360 --> 01:16:30.460
the most popular version is
referred to as Polyak averaging,

01:16:30.460 --> 01:16:33.600
where it's basically a moving
average of the parameters.

01:16:33.600 --> 01:16:36.460
So if you wish exact
same intuitions apply.

01:16:36.460 --> 01:16:39.360
But the update of our
target network, Q phi

01:16:39.360 --> 01:16:44.140
is not done in a so kind
of harsh step fashion way.

01:16:44.140 --> 01:16:49.400
But phi will be a moving average
of previous versions of theta.

01:16:52.860 --> 01:16:54.880
But this is exactly the idea.

01:16:54.880 --> 01:16:59.100
And so you see that I would say
these two contributions were

01:16:59.100 --> 01:17:03.820
basically key in making
deep reinforcement learning

01:17:03.820 --> 01:17:06.000
algorithms work in practice.

01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:09.960
Does this make sense?

01:17:09.960 --> 01:17:11.160
Any questions here?

01:17:17.820 --> 01:17:21.340
So this wraps up,
concludes our discussion

01:17:21.340 --> 01:17:23.300
of value based methods.

01:17:23.300 --> 01:17:26.740
Next week we'll be discussing
policy optimization based

01:17:26.740 --> 01:17:28.360
methods, again, model free.

01:17:28.360 --> 01:17:30.260
And then we'll move
for our last lecture

01:17:30.260 --> 01:17:33.460
on model-based reinforcement
learning methods.

01:17:33.460 --> 01:17:35.410
See you next week.

